<speak>
<s>This is your briefing for <say-as interpret-as="date" format="dmy">15042026</say-as>.</s>
<break time="1s"/>
<prosody rate="95%">
<s>The Hormuz blockade has produced the worst oil supply disruption in recorded history.</s> <s>The strait carries twenty percent of the world's crude, and its closure since February has swung global demand forecasts from a surplus of six hundred forty thousand barrels a day to a deficit of eighty thousand — the first negative swing the International Energy Agency has ever documented.</s> <s>Oil fell six percent after Washington signaled that talks with Tehran could resume within days, but the strait remains shut, and a two hundred thirty-eight million dollar US Navy surveillance drone that crashed over it last week still hasn't been explained — the Pentagon waited five days to even confirm the loss.</s>
</prosody>
<p>
<s>In politics.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Italy has terminated its twenty-one-year defense agreement with Israel after Israeli forces struck Italian UN peacekeepers in southern Lebanon.</s> <s>Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni — a leader who'd built close ties with Tel Aviv — ended the pact and dispatched her foreign minister to Beirut to demand Israeli withdrawal.</s> <s>It's the first time a NATO ally has severed a bilateral defense agreement with Israel, and so far no European government has followed her lead.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Eighty-eight medics have been killed by Israeli forces in southern Lebanon, according to the country's Health Ministry, which has documented a hundred and fourteen separate attacks on medical services.</s> <s>Global data shows Israel was responsible for fifty-three percent of all attacks on medical facilities worldwide and sixty-seven percent of all attacks on health workers in twenty twenty-three and twenty twenty-four.</s> <s>The International Criminal Court holds jurisdiction — but not a single state has triggered a referral.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>US Southern Command has killed a hundred and sixty-eight people at sea in forty-eight strikes since September — without congressional authorization, without charges, and without trial.</s> <s>Targets are designated narco-terrorists under a framework Congress never voted on and no court has tested, with rules that bypass judicial review entirely.</s> <s>President Trump pledged Monday to use the same targeting system to enforce the Hormuz blockade — extending extrajudicial killing from the Caribbean to the Persian Gulf.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Xi Jinping has presented a four-point peace proposal for the Iran conflict while Chinese vessels keep transiting the strait in open defiance of the US Navy blockade.</s> <s>Beijing called the blockade a return to the law of the jungle — language that positions China as a defender of international maritime order while securing its own energy supply lines through the very waterway in dispute.</s> <s>Iran hasn't responded, and whether Beijing's mediation serves peace or its own crude imports is the question Washington doesn't want asked.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Iran has sent a formal letter to UN Secretary-General Guterres demanding war reparations from five Arab states — Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Qatar, the United Arab Emirates, and Jordan — for hosting US forces that struck Iranian territory in February.</s> <s>Tehran doesn't expect payment; the demand forces each government to publicly own its role in enabling the strikes, turning a financial claim into a diplomatic instrument.</s> <s>Gulf capitals now face a choice between silence that looks like admission and a denial that puts them at odds with Washington.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Bangladesh's interim government won its reform referendum by a commanding margin — then stalled on implementation.</s> <s>Post-revolution governments routinely win mandates and delay restructuring, because the institutions they'd reform are the same institutions they now control.</s> <s>The Bangladesh Nationalist Party has escalated to walkouts and street protests, and the crowds that toppled the last government haven't forgotten how it's done.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>On the economy.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Pacific island nations — among the most fuel-dependent economies on earth — are facing a connectivity crisis as Hormuz-driven oil prices surge across supply chains that were fragile to begin with.</s> <s>Every litre of diesel powering flights, cargo ships, and electricity generators is imported, and there's no domestic alternative on any island.</s> <s>China and Australia are competing to fill the energy vacuum, but for governments with no leverage, choosing between two great powers isn't a strategy — it's a gamble.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>India's benchmark index rose one point four percent overnight as crude prices cooled — a direct lifeline for an economy that imports more than eighty percent of its oil.</s> <s>Lower crude shrinks the current account deficit and eases inflation, two pressures that've weighed on Indian markets for months and driven foreign investors to pull billions of rupees.</s> <s>Whether falling oil reverses the capital flight or merely slows it is the number Mumbai's watching today.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>In science.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>Rice has hit its evolutionary thermal limit — temperatures are now rising faster than the nine thousand years of gradual warming that shaped the crop's heat tolerance in the first place.</s> <s>South and Southeast Asia, home to three and a half billion people who depend on rice as a staple, is warming faster than the global average.</s> <s>The grain that feeds nearly half the world is running out of biological room to adapt, and no breeding programme has closed the gap.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Nine children have died in Taunsa, in Pakistan's Punjab province, after a hospital spread HIV to at least three hundred and thirty-one young patients through unsafe medical practices.</s> <s>Punjab's government suspended the medical superintendent last March — then quietly reappointed him to another children's clinic in a different district.</s> <s>BBC undercover footage shows the same practices continuing unchanged, with officials confirming nineteen new cases in the most recent count.</s>
</p>
<p>
<s>In technology.</s><break time="400ms"/>
<s>An AI system has completed a full corporate network attack autonomously for the first time, chaining dozens of exploitation steps into a complete breach from reconnaissance to data access without human guidance.</s> <s>Previous models could handle isolated tasks — scanning a port, writing a payload — but this one planned and executed an entire intrusion sequence on its own.</s> <s>A competing lab released a parallel cyber-capable model the same week, and no regulatory framework in any country governs either of them.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>Japan's Ground Self-Defense Force has established two new offices dedicated to drone and unmanned warfare — driven not by military doctrine but by a demographic crisis that's left it unable to recruit enough soldiers to fill existing units.</s> <s>It's the first major military to institutionalize unmanned operations because it literally ran out of people.</s> <s>What Ukraine demonstrated on the battlefield through improvisation, Japan is now embedding into permanent military bureaucracy.</s>
<break time="600ms"/>
<s>A ransomware operation called JanaWare has targeted Turkish systems exclusively since twenty twenty, using locale settings and IP geolocation to ensure it activates only on Turkish devices.</s> <s>Researchers haven't identified the operators, but a six-year targeting window against a single nation's population points to a persistent campaign — not opportunistic crime.</s> <s>The cybercriminal ecosystem is fragmenting along national lines, and Turkey's become the proving ground.</s>
</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<prosody rate="90%">
<s>That's your briefing.</s>
</prosody>
</speak>